{"id":"meta-earnings-q3-2026","title":"META earnings — Meta Platforms, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-21","html":"<p>Management sees strong ad demand on both volume and price and will keep investing aggressively in AI, and the thing that could spoil it is that legal judgments and settlements have grown into multi-billion-dollar cash bills.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-10-21. Next report: not found (Webull lists no date yet). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Revenue +26%; ad impressions +10% and average price per ad +14%.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised on spending. 2026 capital spending $135–145B (low end up from $130B); 2026 total expenses $175–179B, up from $165–169B, a rise about the size of the legal charge. Q4 revenue $73–76B.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> legal bills turning into cash: a $9.89B charge this quarter, an agreement with state attorneys general of up to about $18.59B, and a $25B New Mexico penalty under appeal.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> no lean. Price $628.77 closed just under the 200-day ($630.71); the Nov 5 low at $583.80 is the floor.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $64.60B against a $63.41B estimate, and above the top of July's $61–64B guide.</li><li>GAAP EPS $3.83 against a $5.42 estimate. The miss is the $9.89B legal charge; without it the company gives $7.40. Compare like with like: the estimate did not include the charge.</li><li>Free cash flow was negative $2.1B after $34.9B of capital spending (including finance lease principal).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> revenue $73–76B.</li><li><strong>Ad demand:</strong> strong on both volume and price.</li><li><strong>Compute:</strong> computing power is what limits the consumer AI product, which has not launched globally. The target is a more than tenfold cost-efficiency gain within a year.</li><li><strong>Costs:</strong> &quot;we expect total serving costs to rise.&quot; (Susan Li, CFO). Expenses grew 68% including the charge, driven by infrastructure and third-party AI usage.</li><li><strong>New business:</strong> an enterprise product line to sell AI capacity and coding tools to businesses.</li><li><strong>Reality Labs:</strong> operating loss $18–19B in 2026, narrowing to about $15B in 2027.</li><li><strong>2027:</strong> no revenue, expense or capital spending figures yet; detail due on the Q4 call.</li><li><strong>Headcount:</strong> 66,775, down 15% from a year ago after a May reduction.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Legal exposure.</strong> The state attorneys general agreement runs to about $18.59B (about $12.7B over 10 years; about $5.3B contingent and not accrued).</li><li><strong>The New Mexico penalty.</strong> A court ordered a $25B civil penalty on Oct 21, with 15% post-judgment interest. Meta has accrued an undisclosed amount and intends to appeal.</li><li><strong>Tax.</strong> An IRS notice asserts an additional $15.89B.</li><li><strong>Serving costs.</strong> Rising as AI use grows, ahead of the revenue from it.</li><li><strong>Commitments.</strong> Leases not yet started about $355.7B; other non-cancellable commitments $454.5B; off-balance-sheet data center ventures with a maximum exposure of $68.4B.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-legal-bills-turning-into-cash-both-ways\">The big worry: legal bills turning into cash, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Winning or cutting the New Mexico penalty on appeal. Meta had argued for no more than $3.4B, and its legal chief said the company is &quot;confident we will prevail on appeal&quot; (PYMNTS, citing Reuters).</li><li>Court approval of the state attorneys general agreement on the stated terms, which spreads most of the cash over 10 years.</li><li>Settling the remaining youth cases early. Meta settled a California state-court trial on Oct 30, two days after it began (Drugwatch timeline).</li><li>The ad business staying this strong: at $64.6B of revenue a quarter, a spread-out bill is affordable.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Time. At 15% a year, interest on $25B is about $3.75B a year (my arithmetic) while the appeal runs. The judgment as reported has no stay or bond provision (The Deep Dive, third-party).</li><li>More verdicts. The federal case group held 3,824 suits as of Oct 1, and school-district bellwether trials are scheduled for February 2027 (Tech Policy Press).</li><li>The contingent $5.3B of the state agreement becoming due, or other states pressing copycat penalty claims.</li><li>The IRS claim going against it at the same time as the build needs cash: free cash flow is already negative.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>It has the ad cash machine, but it is spending all of it: capital spending of $135–145B this year, no buybacks in 2026, and notes outstanding up to $84.0B from $59.0B.</li><li>The new non-voting Class C share (below) gives it a way to raise money without touching control, which helps pay bills but dilutes owners.</li><li>Against peers: Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon carry tax and antitrust disputes too, but none has a single court penalty this large on the table.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> $135–145B in 2026; 2027 detail on the Q4 call.</li><li><strong>Buybacks:</strong> none in 2026 ($26.2B in the same period of 2025).</li><li><strong>Dividend:</strong> $0.525 a share.</li><li><strong>Debt and equity:</strong> $25.0B of notes sold in May; Class C shares arrive Jan 19, 2027 as a one-for-one stock dividend, with capital raising among the stated purposes.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Mark Zuckerberg, founder, chairman and CEO. The roles are combined; the lead independent director is Robert Kimmitt.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Susan Li, since 2022. Dina Powell McCormick became president and vice chairman in 2026.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> no. The proxy says only that the board oversees CEO succession planning.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> a &quot;controlled company&quot; under Nasdaq rules. Zuckerberg holds 60.8% of the votes through Class B shares with 10 votes each (2026 proxy), on about 13% of the economics (Decrypt). The board opposed a shareholder proposal for one share, one vote.</li><li><strong>Class C:</strong> Meta says the stock dividend changes no one's voting power. As reported by Decrypt, issuing or selling non-voting shares for cash, pay or deals would not dilute his control; Fast Company reports he may sell 20% of his non-voting shares under a &quot;Founder Alignment Agreement&quot;.</li><li><strong>Recent changes:</strong> Joel Kaplan, chief global affairs officer, is to leave in 2027 with no successor named (first reported by Bloomberg, per The Next Web). Investigations of executives: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> nearly everything. One founder holds the chair, the CEO job and a voting majority, no successor is named, and the new share class is built to keep it that way while the company raises money. Of the five cloud names this is the most concentrated.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"META\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"META · daily · my levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">META · daily · my levels</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\